hey David given that there's been so  much talk about voting in part because  of the midterm election a few days ago I  want to ask you a question what is worse  voting ignorant or not voting at all  because you always talk about the  importance of voting but do we really  want people who don't know anything  going out to the polls so this is mean  it's the framing is not unfair but it's  sort of a deceptive framing no one is  advocating that I've seen going out and  voting by shooting darts so to speak  randomly picking candidates to vote for  that I would argue really doesn't help  anybody but the case I would make is  that even an uninformed voter turnout of  90% would force politicians to pay more  attention to what people want  even if they don't always know what they  want or they want things that don't make  sense then when you have fifty percent  voter turnout which quite frankly we  already have filled with ignorant people  and malignantly misinformed people  anyway in other words the idea that the  options are to go from we either stay at  50 percent voter turnout and everybody's  informed or we go to 90 percent voter  turnout but everybody is uninformed all  of a sudden we already have a huge  percentage of the voting public  completely uninformed about the issues  and the candidates you you really barely  need to do any research to be better  informed than random choice doesn't mean  I'll agree with you but if we have  representative democracy it should be  representative of as many votes as  possible so is the idea to convince  people to vote uninformed like is that  the idea behind just vote everybody vote  of course not but if voting is something  everyone or almost everyone does  by sheer osmosis colloquially speaking  not literal osmosis the level to which  people are informed on average is going  to go up okay and I've actually had  periods where I started thinking through  ideas like well what about voting tests  you should at least have some level of  knowledge in order to vote and obviously  that stuff would would be illegal it  would never fly and I think it would  also kind of be antithetical to the  spirit of democracy that the the framers  of the Constitution envisioned but now I  realize that absent anything else absent  a more or less informed voting public  like putting all of that stuff aside the  more votes that are cast the the more  people that vote the better off we are  overall and so the I think it's a false  question to even say do we really want  more people voting if they're not  informed and and additionally I would I  would further that case by saying the  following if everybody voted it's  extraordinarily difficult for me to  imagine that you would have a less  informed population because right now  because only half the country votes by  sheer chance of course depends where you  are a whole bunch of the people you  encounter every day are non voters  meaning they are often less engaged in  the political process and less  knowledgeable if we had 90 percent voter  turnout even if initially you had a lot  of people voting uninformed the fact  that more people are participating will  increase the likelihood that you're  interacting with people every day who  are part of the political process that  will lead to conversation and that will  inevitably lead to people who are more  engaged aware and informed it doesn't  mean we will agree with the conclusions  they come to but that's sort of a second  layer argument and the most important  thing is if we want our elected  representatives to be accountable to us  the more of us that vote the more  accountable they would conceivably have  to be at least as a founding premise and  then of course you have to figure out  will anyone actually hold them  accountable but I think that it is a  false choice to say is it better for  more people to vote if everybody's  voting uninformed it's not the way it  would work it's not actually what would  happen in practice if you disagree with  me on this one I do want to hear from  you
 

